  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 AM EDT Tue Aug 4 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor imagery and RAP analysis reveal \nbroad troughing over the Northern Plains and into the Upper Midwest, \nwith an embedded shortwave pivoting northeastward out of the \nDakotas. At the surface, an associated cold front is directed from \njust west of Lake Nipigon southwestward through MN, with a batch of \nshowers and storms reaching the western shores of Lake Superior. \nCloser to home, skies remain mostly clear over the UP, though cloud \ncover is beginning to thicken over the west. Temperatures hover in \nthe 60s across most of the area, but are still in the lower 70s \nacross the far western UP and the Keweenaw; temperatures should only \nfall a few more degrees tonight with the warming airmass and \nincreasing cloud cover from west to east.\n\nShowers and storms finally reach the western UP towards daybreak as \nthe cold front sweeps through, but CAMs continue to indicate that \nthey quickly diminish with eastward as they encounter a dry and \nstable airmass over the central UP. However, surface-based \ndestabilization ahead of the front, over the eastern UP, will help \nconvection ramp up again there during the afternoon as the front \ncontinues its eastward trek. MUCAPE generally stays below 750 J/kg, \nso any storms are not expected to be strong/severe. That said, there \nis some question as to whether surface based instability can even \ndevelop given model soundings depicting a warm nose around 800-850 \nmb, so coverage of storms is expected to remain isolated at best. \nHighs range from the low 70s west, where FROPA happens earliest, to \nlow 80s east.\n\nWeak ridging builds in the wake of the front for Wednesday, setting \nup a dry and seasonable day with highs mainly in the 70s. Another \nshortwave trough rotates through Thursday, but with a lack of any \nnotable moisture, another dry day is expected. Temperatures are \nexpected to be on a general warming trend late week into the weekend \nas low-level southwesterly flow develops. Thunderstorm potential \nenters the picture again by the weekend as strong quasi-zonal flow \ndevelops between Hudson Bay low and Four Corners high. Unclear \nwhether the reservoir of greater instability will get this far north.",
